\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x - 4\\), we start by considering the behavior of the left-hand side and the right-hand side.\n\nFirst, let's denote the left-hand side of the equation by \\(L(x)\\) and the right-hand side by \\(R(x)\\), so we have:\n\\[ L(x) = \\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} \\]\n\\[ R(x) = 2010x - 4 \\]\n\nThe function \\(L(x)\\) is a sum of rational functions, and it has vertical asymptotes at \\(x = \\pm 1, \\pm \\sqrt{2}, \\pm \\sqrt{3}, \\pm 2\\). On the other hand, \\(R(x)\\) is a linear function. \n\nTo find the sum of all complex solutions, we can use the fact that the sum of the roots of a polynomial equation \\(P(x) = 0\\) is given by the negative of the coefficient of the second highest degree term divided by the leading coefficient (by Vieta's formulas). However, we need to transform our equation into a polynomial form.\n\nLet's assume \\(x\\) is a complex number that satisfies the equation. If we multiply both sides by the denominator \\((x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)\\), we get a polynomial equation in \\(x\\). The polynomial will be of degree 8, and the sum of the roots of this polynomial will be the sum of the complex solutions of the original equation.\n\nSince the polynomial will have terms of the form \\(a_8 x^8 + a_7 x^7 + \\cdots + a_0\\), the sum of the roots (by Vieta's formulas) is given by \\(-a_7 / a_8\\). However, the coefficient of \\(x^7\\) will come from the linear term \\(2010x\\) on the right-hand side when we expand and collect terms. This means the coefficient of \\(x^7\\) will be 0 (since there is no \\(x^7\\) term on the left-hand side after expansion), and the sum of the roots is 0.\n\nTherefore, the sum of all complex solutions of the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
